## 4. Shared Tool Gateway architecture

Implement one centralized gateway within the existing backend architecture.

Conceptual architecture:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Authorized Agent or Backend Caller] --> B[Shared Tool Gateway]
    B --> C[Request Validation]
    C --> D[Agent Identity and Version Validation]
    D --> E[Organization and Category Authorization]
    E --> F[Tool Registry]
    F --> G[Tool Permission Validation]
    G --> H[Resource Ownership Validation]
    H --> I{Approval Required?}
    I -->|Yes| J[Approval Workflow]
    I -->|No| K[Tool Execution Service]
    J -->|Approved| K
    J -->|Rejected| L[Rejected Result]
    K --> M[Existing GeFlow Business Services]
    M --> N[Standardized Tool Response]
    N --> O[Audit and Execution Trace]
    L --> O
```

This is a logical architecture diagram. Reuse the actual application framework, backend services and infrastructure.

Do not build an unnecessary separate microservice if the existing backend can safely host the gateway.

### Gateway responsibilities

The gateway must:

1. Accept an authenticated internal tool invocation.
2. Validate the caller's identity.
3. Validate the registered agent.
4. Validate the agent configuration version.
5. Validate organization and business context.
6. Validate category permissions.
7. Resolve the requested tool.
8. Check the agent's tool permissions.
9. Validate the tool input schema.
10. Check resource ownership and scope.
11. Enforce approval requirements.
12. Execute only the approved operation.
13. Return a standardized response.
14. Record the execution event.

No individual agent should be able to bypass these controls by calling internal business services directly.

