## 5. Centralized Tool Registry

Implement a persistent or code-defined tool registry using the existing architecture.

First inspect whether an equivalent registry already exists.

The registry will describe which tools are available, their input/output contracts, permissions and execution policies.

### 5.1 Logical tool record

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| tool_id | Permanent unique tool identifier |
| tool_name | Human-readable tool name |
| description | Purpose of the tool |
| category | Functional tool grouping |
| module_id | Associated GeFlow module |
| version | Tool contract version |
| status | Tool availability status |
| input_schema | Input validation schema |
| output_schema | Expected response schema |
| required_permissions | Required permission references |
| allowed_agent_ids | Agent access policy |
| approval_policy | Whether approval is required |
| execution_policy | Execution restrictions |
| timeout_policy | Execution timeout configuration |
| idempotency_policy | Duplicate execution handling |
| created_at | Creation timestamp |
| updated_at | Last update timestamp |

Actual field types and storage must follow the existing database conventions.

### 5.2 Tool identity

Use stable tool identifiers.

Examples:

- `inventory.product.get`
- `inventory.stock.get`
- `inventory.stock.adjust`
- `sales.invoice.get`
- `sales.invoice.create`
- `purchases.purchase.get`
- `customers.customer.get`
- `suppliers.supplier.get`
- `reports.sales.summary`

These are proposed identifiers, not proof that the corresponding business operations already exist.

Register only tools backed by verified existing functionality or explicitly approved new implementations.

### 5.3 Tool lifecycle

Support logical statuses:

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| draft | Tool is being prepared |
| active | Tool is available to authorized callers |
| inactive | Tool is temporarily unavailable |
| deprecated | Tool is being phased out |
| archived | Tool is retained for historical reference |

Inactive or archived tools must not execute.

A tool must not become available merely because a caller knows its identifier.

