## 11. Tool execution engine

Implement a controlled execution dispatcher inside the gateway.

### 11.1 Execution sequence

For every invocation:

1. Receive the internal request.
2. Generate or validate a request correlation ID.
3. Establish trusted execution context.
4. Authenticate the caller.
5. Validate agent identity and version.
6. Resolve the tool from the registry.
7. Validate tool lifecycle status.
8. Validate tool input.
9. Enforce organization and category authorization.
10. Validate agent-specific tool permissions.
11. Validate resource ownership.
12. Check approval requirements.
13. Apply rate limits and execution policies.
14. Execute the approved backend operation.
15. Validate the returned result.
16. Produce a standardized response.
17. Record the execution audit event.

If any required authorization check fails, the operation must not execute.

### 11.2 Existing business services

Where possible, tools must call existing GeFlow business services.

Example:

```text
Shared Tool Gateway
        |
        v
Inventory Tool Adapter
        |
        v
Existing Inventory Service
        |
        v
Existing Database Layer
```

Do not duplicate business logic inside tool handlers.

Do not create a separate inventory or POS implementation for AI agents.

All existing business validation and transaction rules must remain effective.

### 11.3 No unrestricted database access

Agents must not receive:

- Raw database credentials.
- Direct unrestricted database connections.
- Arbitrary SQL execution.
- Unrestricted ORM access.
- Database administration capabilities.
- Privileged service-role credentials.

All business data operations must go through approved backend service methods.

